// wdoc section: Themes.
page wdoc_themes { sites = [:reference]
  h1 "Themes"

  p "A theme is a complete colour palette plus the rules that map it onto every built-in element — page background, links, headings, code, charts, callouts, tables, inline emphasis. Selecting a theme in one line recolours the whole site."

  h2 "Selecting a built-in theme"

  p "Set `theme = <name>` on a `site`. Seven built-in palettes ship, each with co-ordinated dark and light variants. `theme_toggle = true` adds a light/dark toggle button to the chrome."

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
site mysite {
  default_template = :book
  theme            = tokyonight
  accent           = :cyan
  theme_toggle     = true
}
WCL
  }

  h2 "Built-in themes"

  table {
    rows:
      | "Theme"        | "Notes" |
      | "`nord`"       | "Cool blue-grey, the default" |
      | "`gruvbox`"    | "Warm retro" |
      | "`solarized`"  | "Classic Solarized" |
      | "`catppuccin`" | "Soft pastel" |
      | "`tokyonight`" | "Vivid night palette" |
      | "`kanagawa`"   | "Muted with hints of indigo" |
      | "`everforest`" | "Green forest tones" |
  }

  h2 "Accent colour"

  p "Independently of the theme, `accent = :cyan` (or `:red`/`:orange`/`:yellow`/`:green`/`:blue`/`:purple`/`:pink`) picks the hue used for links and current-chapter highlights. Default is `:blue`."

  h2 "Custom themes"

  p "Themes are ordinary values. Build your own with `let mine = ColourTheme::Of { name dark light }` (each side a `Palette::Of { … }`) and set `theme = mine` on the site. The 18 palette roles are documented in `lib/theme.wcl`."

  callout "Themes set vars, classes win" {
    class = ["info"]
    body  = "Themes emit --wdoc-* custom properties; built-in stylesheets consume them via var(...). User class blocks declared anywhere in the document still take precedence over theme defaults via the cascade, so per-element overrides work as expected."
  }

  p "Next: [Diagrams](wdoc_diagrams)."
}
